    historicalOverview: "Morocco's history stretches from ancient Amazigh societies through Phoenician and Roman contacts to powerful Islamic dynasties. The Almoravid and Almohad empires built states that connected Morocco with large parts of North and West Africa and, at times, Iberia. Later dynasties continued to shape the country's cities, trade routes, and religious institutions.\n\nEuropean pressure intensified in the nineteenth century, especially as France and Spain competed for influence. The Treaty of Fez in 1912 established a French protectorate over most of Morocco, while Spain controlled zones in the north and south. Moroccan resistance and nationalist movements gradually challenged the protectorate system and demanded restored sovereignty.\n\nIndependence in 1956 returned Morocco to full international sovereignty while preserving the monarchy. The reigns of Mohammed V, Hassan II, and Mohammed VI have seen major changes in government, infrastructure, and economic development. Modern Morocco combines Arabic, Amazigh, African, Mediterranean, and Atlantic influences and remains an important bridge between Europe and the wider African region."
